  <reporting-org ref="NL-KVK-40409352" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Humanitarian Design Relay</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2016 the Netherlands Red Cross partnered with the Faculty of Industrial Design Engineering of Delft University of Technology to bring human-centered design into humanitarian aid. The partnership is a simple collaborative design process to enable young people to influence the future of humanitarian.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">N/A</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Partner national societies and communities</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross and the Faculty of Industrial Design Engineering of Delft University partnered for two important reasons: 
(1) To empower young people to re-imagine humanitarian aid: Working directly with students from Delft University of Technology provides them an opportunity to learn about humanitarian aid AND enables them to apply their design skills to propose changes in humanitarian aid 
(2) To re-design humanitarian aid: By committing to testing concepts designed by young people and integrating those that work, in the end results in the delivery of improved products or services for humanitarian aid by the Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The objective of this project was to solidify the partnership with TUD-IDE and other partners in order to design improved or new products, services or approaches for our international humanitarian assistance. The aim of the partnership is intended to have effect at two key levels: 1) Internally, to assist the IA and National Clusters to &#x2018;do things differently&#x2019; and 2) externally to work with NS partners supported by NLRC and IFRC to improve their humanitarian service delivery towards crisis affected people.</narrative>
  </description>
